# Measured per-site training throughput, in SEQUENCES PER SECOND for the whole
# site at its normal rank count and local_batch_size, with accumulation = 1.
#
# What this is for
# ----------------
# A Flower round is a barrier: it cannot close until the slowest site delivers.
# On heterogeneous hardware the fast site therefore finishes its H steps and then
# sits still. Measured on this federation: Snellius did 44s of work inside a 172s
# round and idled the other 128s -- 74% of every round, on hardware that was
# being billed for all of it. Across the campaign that idle is the single
# largest line in the compute bill (CAMPAIGN.md section 6 note: the federated
# arms cost 4.6-5.3x their centralized equivalents for the same tokens).
#
# `scripts/titan/run_train.sh` reads this file when PWW_BALANCE=1 and derives
# each site's PWW_GRAD_ACCUM so that every site's phase takes about the same
# wall-clock. The fast site then spends its spare time on larger microbatches
# instead of on nothing.
#
# Why accumulation rather than a per-site H: raising H on the fast site also
# fills the round, but multiplies its drift and desynchronises the LR schedule.
# Accumulation leaves H, drift, the schedule and peak memory all unchanged --
# see the long comment above GRAD_ACCUM in run_train.sh.
#
# How to (re)measure
# ------------------
#     scripts/titan/calibrate_throughput.sh                  # on a login node or in a job
#     scripts/titan/calibrate_throughput.sh --write          # appends/updates this file
#
# Re-measure when the hardware, the container, the model flavor, seq_len or
# local_batch_size changes. The numbers below are specific to Qwen3 0.6B at
# seq_len 2048, local_batch_size 8, bfloat16, selective activation checkpointing.
#
# Two numbers per site, because throughput alone is not enough
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# What has to be equalised is the time of one optimiser STEP, and sites do not
# run the same batch per step -- each contributes ranks x local_batch_size:
#
#     step_time = batch_per_step / throughput
#
#     Snellius   32 seq/step / 89.8 seq/s = 0.356 s
#     LUMI       64 seq/step / 38.2 seq/s = 1.675 s     -> 4.7x, not 2.4x
#
# Balancing on throughput alone would have given Snellius 2x and left most of
# the idle in place. Both numbers are therefore recorded, and run_train.sh
# divides them.
#
# PWW_TPUT_<SITE>  sequences/second for the whole site, accumulation = 1
# PWW_BATCH_<SITE> sequences per optimiser step, accumulation = 1
#                  (= dp_ranks x training.local_batch_size)
#
# A site missing either value is treated as unknown and left at accumulation 1,
# which is exactly the behaviour before this file existed.

# Measured from all_logs/pww-snellius-titan-full-25742788.out
# Snellius, 4 x H100, gpu_h100. 4 ranks x 8 = 32 seq/step, 0.356s per step.
PWW_TPUT_SNELLIUS=89.8
PWW_BATCH_SNELLIUS=32

# Measured from all_logs/pww-lumi-titan-full-21323379.out
# LUMI, 8 x MI250X GCD, standard-g. 8 ranks x 8 = 64 seq/step, 1.675s per step.
PWW_TPUT_LUMI=38.2
PWW_BATCH_LUMI=64
